column30:
TagSetNominationsController.request_noncanonical_info code is currently:

{code:ruby}
def request_noncanonical_info
    if @tag_set_nomination.character_nominations.any? {|tn| !tn.parented && tn.parent_tagname.blank?} ||
      @tag_set_nomination.relationship_nominations.any? {|tn| !tn.parented && tn.parent_tagname.blank?}
      
      flash[:notice] += ts(" Please consider editing to add fandoms to any of your non-canonical tags!")
    end
end
{code}
This noncanonical state can't be triggered through the UI, so the flash message code path is never reached. This noncanonical state is also already prevented via validations at the model-level (e.g. CastNomination.known_fandom). Because this code is unused, it can be removed.

This came up during AO3-4830 PR review (https://github.com/otwcode/otwarchive/pull/2728). Creating a separate JIRA issue to keep this code separate.

*Testing Note:* This should not change functionality. To test, ensure functionality around creating and updating tag nominations with or without fandom noms is unchanged.

column140:
10/Mar/17 3:39 PM;557058:443ab871-4096-403e-bd6f-f3534fe417f0;This was reverted, so setting back to In Progress and removing from this release. 

Release notes for reuse:
{code:java}
<li>[<a href="https://otwarchive.atlassian.net/browse/AO3-4858">AO3-4858</a>] - Whenever a work is posted, we count how many times each tag on it has been used on the entire Archive. Because some tags have been used on hundreds of thousands of works, it can take several minutes to perform the count -- and while we're counting, the database has to be locked. This meant that posting and updating works was often very slow, sometimes resulting in 503 errors. To reduce the number of errors and speed up posting and editing, we've implemented a new caching method for keeping track of how many times very large tags have been used.</li>
{code}

